## 2026-11-01 — Task 4: Actions build-cost investigation

Inspected actual API job timings/logs for the three latest completed main runs with successful native-build and Park Linux jobs: 2026-10-30 and twice on 2026-10-31. Their build, Park and helper workflow files are byte-identical to the current checkout; Ubuntu24.04/Rust1.96 are unchanged. Source revisions differ: this is attribution, not a controlled before/after benchmark. Cancelled/in-progress/early-failed samples are excluded from these medians.

| Measured step | Median | Range |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Park compiler/protocol checks | 71m26s | 62m36s–76m34s |
| Cargo-reported preparation within that step | 70m21.51s | 61m19.21s–75m25.93s |
| Sum of reported libtest execution | 26.79s | 26.11s–27.25s |
| Native release compilation | 6m33s | 5m11s–51m12s |
| Release cache restore / save | 3m03s / 1m59s | 2m12s–3m10s / 1m52s–2m14s |
| Park validation-helper preparation | 10m04s | 9m59s–10m53s |

Finding: the largest observed repeated cost is Park checking/test compilation. Each log has 3,792 Checking/Compiling entries (not unique crates). Cargo preparation accounts for about 98% of the step; this is reported wall time, not compiler CPU attribution. Park's Linux job restores/saves no Cargo artifact cache and tests the source-timestamp script without applying it to restored artifacts. Nix tool/library substitution does not provide compiled project artifacts. The release job demonstrably restores its prefix-matched cache and unchanged source timestamps; unique save keys or cache-hit=false do not mean no cache was restored.

At investigation time, the candidate was compatible Park dev/test artifact reuse while preserving the commands. The later owner instruction replaces those Actions suites with compilation checks; see the consolidation report below. The API snapshot listed 12 caches totaling 9.28 GB and no binary-v2/park-validation entries; the configured quota and disappearance/eviction cause are unverified. No cache was deleted. The investigation itself changed no workflows and claimed no speedup. Main release publication preceded the Park job.

Sources: [2026-10-31 Park check](https://github.com/lince-social/lince/actions/runs/41204290581/job/124548726549), [2026-10-31 Park check](https://github.com/lince-social/lince/actions/runs/41148219014/job/124382451841), [2026-10-30 Park check](https://github.com/lince-social/lince/actions/runs/41051597656/job/124072943905). API records, logs, workflow hashes, exclusions, cache snapshot and calculated results: `target/build-iteration/actions-cost/result.json` and adjacent receipts. Task 4's investigation is complete; hosted validation of the implemented caches remains task 5.

## 2026-11-01 — Actions consolidation and cache validation

| Change or experiment | Result and decision |
| --- | --- |
| One workflow per supported Actions platform | Keep `build.yml` for Linux Lince/Park and `android.yml` for Android. Remove the separate Park, validation, probe, Transfer, Android-smoke and disabled Instinct workflows. No automated test suites or emulators remain. |
| Linux checks versus packages | Pull requests/dev use locked Cargo checks for native Lince/Park, tools/clients and headless Lince. Main/version tags build the optimized native package. Manual runs choose checks or a package; publication additionally requires explicit selection. Existing package checks, signing and main/tag publication remain. |
| Android checks versus packages | Automated ARM64 Rust checks; manual ARM64/x86_64/both packages. Install Java/Gradle only for packaging and skip the duplicate preliminary check when building a package. Signed release APK/Play bundle choices retain main/ARM64 restrictions and signature/alignment verification. NDK/release settings remain unchanged. |
| Scoped compiled-input caches | Implement compatible Linux check/release and Android ABI/mode caches with content-aware timestamps. Stable dependency keys avoid per-commit archive churn. Native installer and Android Gradle flags are fingerprinted; first use of the new namespace is cold. Final executables and signing outputs are excluded. |
| Local invalidation tests | All 18 pass in 8.055 s, including real Cargo unchanged-source reuse and recompilation after changed/new/deleted inputs, permissions, compiler flags, lockfile, native tools and build recipes. This validates reuse correctness, not hosted transfer savings. |
| Workflow validation | Actionlint, ShellCheck and diff checks pass; 16 event/platform combinations and signing guards pass. Exactly two workflows remain, with no suite/emulator commands. No remote workflow dispatch or publishing was performed. |

Receipts: `target/build-iteration/actions-consolidation/`. Hosted cold/warm/source-edit timings and cache sizes remain unmeasured until these files run remotely. The old 71m26s Park step included test compilation; it is not a like-for-like baseline for the new check-only workflow. No hosted percentage improvement is claimed.

## Promising result awaiting adoption checks

Earlier R59: native Interface512 shared edit/restoration cycles improved 105.549 → 79.254 s (24.91%); setup cost 214.60 s. Native control recorded 450 passes/10 failures and a 600 s timeout. Four failures were corrected socket-path fixture errors; six behavior cases and the post-catalogue timeout remained unresolved in that run. Keep Interface256 until a valid runtime comparison passes. These are validation blockers for this candidate, not independent build optimizations. The prior failures involved two customization fixtures and Practice rows, mail permissions, vocabulary confirmation and shader completion; reproduce/report any remaining blocker without expanding this plan into product repairs. This is distinct from the rejected Park test-only 512 setting.

## Not queued without evidence

Storage/RAM/no-CoW, Crane packaging reuse, narrower Nix sources, feature unification, asset separation, broader nextest and further compiler flags lack a demonstrated large common-workflow opportunity. Storage requires owned file-I/O attribution; package reuse requires repeated dependency recompilation; source filtering requires generator/path/input inventory and complete installed Trails content. Initial inspection is not a speedup.

Simulation policy separation and Action serialization are architectural proposals, not proven build wins. A simulation prototype must preserve automatic enforcement, all nine roots, no-Cell execution, worker placement and session/replay/campaign/preview identity binding. Action changes must preserve its live schema, strict input rejection and optimized behavior. Complete effective-input identity must cover resolved features/config/profile, included/generated/local source and native tools/flags; retain fail-closed behavior and avoid Cargo private fingerprints as a stable API. These contracts gate affected proposals; they do not justify speculative rewrites.

Remote infrastructure, build-system migration, recurring automation and PGO/BOLT require their own concrete objective/resources. They are not prerequisites for the current task queue.

## Rules for a selected experiment

Record hypothesis, exact pins/commands, source guards, useful-saving threshold, bounded samples/time, setup/cache state and rollback before changing code. Benchmark one factor at a time, sequentially, with at least three balanced uncontaminated samples per side for a performance claim. Keep all samples, ranges and exclusions; separate checking, compilation/linking, loading, execution and one-time setup.

Require useful complete-workflow gains, preserved correctness/privacy/recovery and final binary performance. Verify relevant source additions/deletions, dependency/features/tools/native invalidation, and the actual normal command before adoption. Full physical/session acceptance is required only where affected. Restore probes/rejected changes and record adopt, scoped opt-in, reject or conditional deferral. Validate interactions in the useful combination; never add separate gains or attribute global pressure to owned builds.
}
